00:30:21.530 --> 00:30:23.390
You need to make judgment calls.

00:30:23.390 --> 00:30:26.930
But sometimes you can use
data in a more effective way,

00:30:26.930 --> 00:30:28.560
and that's kind of our mission.

00:30:28.560 --> 00:30:32.270
So what we want to do is to
help our customers and partners

00:30:32.270 --> 00:30:35.233
organize the world's
health care information.

00:30:35.233 --> 00:30:36.900
There is a lot of
information out there.

00:30:36.900 --> 00:30:39.440
We see data that imaging
data, genomics data,

00:30:39.440 --> 00:30:42.120
is doubling now
every several months.

00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:44.610
So how do we help organize it?

00:30:44.610 --> 00:30:46.160
How do we make it secure?

00:30:46.160 --> 00:30:47.700
And how do we make it useful?

00:30:47.700 --> 00:30:48.200
Right?

00:30:48.200 --> 00:30:50.600
So secure and
accessible, this is

00:30:50.600 --> 00:30:54.380
kind of the core infrastructure
or the core cloud capabilities.

00:30:54.380 --> 00:30:56.210
And then how do
you make it useful?

00:30:56.210 --> 00:30:59.720
This boils down to analytics
and machine learning.

00:30:59.720 --> 00:31:01.340
These are hot themes.

00:31:01.340 --> 00:31:03.710
At every booth you go,
you'll hear about it.

00:31:03.710 --> 00:31:05.150
My goal in this
short presentation

00:31:05.150 --> 00:31:07.040
is to give you a little
bit more specifics.

00:31:07.040 --> 00:31:11.190
What does it mean for us
in the health care space?

00:31:11.190 --> 00:31:13.340
So when we think about
what we are building,

00:31:13.340 --> 00:31:15.230
like if you ask like,
what is Google Cloud

00:31:15.230 --> 00:31:16.260
health care is doing?

00:31:16.260 --> 00:31:20.420
So we think of our offering
as a data platform,

00:31:20.420 --> 00:31:24.980
that is meant to enable
providers, researchers, to ask

00:31:24.980 --> 00:31:28.380
and answer new questions using
the information that they have.

00:31:28.380 --> 00:31:31.410
And we think of this
platform in three layers.

00:31:31.410 --> 00:31:32.820
There is the data platform.

00:31:32.820 --> 00:31:35.060
I'll go into each
layer specifically.

00:31:35.060 --> 00:31:38.720
But there is a data layer, there
is the cloud services layer,

00:31:38.720 --> 00:31:41.400
and then there is the
applications layer.

00:31:41.400 --> 00:31:44.870
So let's talk about
the data layer first.

00:31:44.870 --> 00:31:47.900
So the data layer
basically means that you

00:31:47.900 --> 00:31:49.020
have a lot of information.

00:31:49.020 --> 00:31:51.312
If you're a health care
provider, or health care payer,

00:31:51.312 --> 00:31:53.990
or a research organization,
you have a lot of information

00:31:53.990 --> 00:31:56.660
that your machines, or your
patients is generating.

00:31:56.660 --> 00:31:58.970
You need some sort of way
to organize and manage

00:31:58.970 --> 00:32:01.080
this information in the cloud.

00:32:01.080 --> 00:32:04.130
So what we have built
and released-- right now

00:32:04.130 --> 00:32:06.800
it's in beta-- is
a set of services

00:32:06.800 --> 00:32:10.880
that allows you to manage
standard health care data

00:32:10.880 --> 00:32:13.040
types in a fully managed way.

00:32:13.040 --> 00:32:17.420
So what that means is that
we have a DICOM endpoint that

00:32:17.420 --> 00:32:18.320
runs in the cloud.

00:32:18.320 --> 00:32:21.830
We have a service that
can manage HL7 V2 data.

00:32:21.830 --> 00:32:24.208
We have a services
can manage FHIR data.

00:32:24.208 --> 00:32:26.000
So these are kind of
the fundamental pieces

00:32:26.000 --> 00:32:29.600
of any health care data,
or health care data set,

00:32:29.600 --> 00:32:31.360
or data warehouse.

00:32:31.360 --> 00:32:35.840
Now I'll give this example of a
super high level overview of--

00:32:35.840 --> 00:32:37.480
I wouldn't even call
it architecture,

00:32:37.480 --> 00:32:39.590
but maybe a meta architecture.

00:32:39.590 --> 00:32:42.290
And we showed the demo
at the conference call

00:32:42.290 --> 00:32:45.180
RSNA, which is the radiology
conference in Chicago.

00:32:45.180 --> 00:32:47.690
It happens every year
around Thanksgiving.

00:32:47.690 --> 00:32:50.240
And what we showed there is
basically an off the shelf

00:32:50.240 --> 00:32:54.050
medical imaging device that
connects through the cloud.

00:32:54.050 --> 00:32:56.460
Our cloud can speak
DICOM natively.

00:32:56.460 --> 00:32:58.730
So that means that you can
just connect our cloud,

00:32:58.730 --> 00:33:01.370
and then just any
medical imaging

00:33:01.370 --> 00:33:03.560
modality or medical
imaging device,

00:33:03.560 --> 00:33:05.732
so the data arrives
to the cloud.

00:33:05.732 --> 00:33:07.190
And then from there,
you can either

00:33:07.190 --> 00:33:09.710
visualize it using something
like a zero footprint

00:33:09.710 --> 00:33:12.140
viewer of your
choice, or you can

00:33:12.140 --> 00:33:17.240
run additional computation,
analytics applications, on it.

00:33:17.240 --> 00:33:19.190
In this case, what
we have done, we

00:33:19.190 --> 00:33:22.410
use the tool called AutoML,
Auto Machine Learning.

00:33:22.410 --> 00:33:24.060
I'll talk about it in a second.

00:33:24.060 --> 00:33:26.930
But we train a very
simple AI model

00:33:26.930 --> 00:33:31.880
to separate between a hypoechoic
and a hyperechoic ultrasound

00:33:31.880 --> 00:33:32.720
mass.

00:33:32.720 --> 00:33:34.550
Now what that means
in very simple terms

00:33:34.550 --> 00:33:39.480
is that we were able to separate
water bubbles from air bubbles.

00:33:39.480 --> 00:33:41.510
Now it's a simple
demo, so this was only

00:33:41.510 --> 00:33:44.940
shown for demonstration of
capabilities of the technology.

00:33:44.940 --> 00:33:46.970
But we have right now
customers and partners

00:33:46.970 --> 00:33:50.300
that are using these tools to
develop clinical applications

00:33:50.300 --> 00:33:51.810
on real data.

00:33:51.810 --> 00:33:54.720
So that's the piece I
want to communicate here.

00:33:54.720 --> 00:33:58.180
The other piece is
the public data set.

00:33:58.180 --> 00:34:00.980
So this is especially
relevant for researchers.

00:34:00.980 --> 00:34:03.680
If you're trying to
test a hypothesis,

00:34:03.680 --> 00:34:05.810
then you need to have
access to some data

00:34:05.810 --> 00:34:07.400
to be able to test
your algorithm,

00:34:07.400 --> 00:34:09.170
to test your application.

00:34:09.170 --> 00:34:12.170
Right now there is
a lot of data sets

00:34:12.170 --> 00:34:14.310
that are public, in
the public domain.

00:34:14.310 --> 00:34:17.250
But to get access to them
it's not straightforward.

00:34:17.250 --> 00:34:18.530
You have to download them.

00:34:18.530 --> 00:34:21.560
It's like oftentimes, it's
like large, terabyte scale data

00:34:21.560 --> 00:34:22.370
sets.

00:34:22.370 --> 00:34:26.659
You have to make sure that
your tools are compliant

00:34:26.659 --> 00:34:29.510
and they're compatible
with these data sets.

00:34:29.510 --> 00:34:31.139
So we want to
simplify the tasks.

00:34:31.139 --> 00:34:34.159
So we have taken data sets
that are already public,

00:34:34.159 --> 00:34:36.739
in the medical imaging
domain, in the EHR domain,

00:34:36.739 --> 00:34:40.219
in the genomics domain, and
we host them on our platform,

00:34:40.219 --> 00:34:42.362
so that you can come
and test out your tools

00:34:42.362 --> 00:34:43.820
on the data that
is already public,

00:34:43.820 --> 00:34:45.230
in a more straightforward way.

00:34:48.050 --> 00:34:50.616
So let's say your data is in
the cloud, kind of mentally.

00:34:50.616 --> 00:34:51.699
Your data is in the cloud.

00:34:51.699 --> 00:34:53.770
The question is
like, OK now what?

00:34:53.770 --> 00:34:57.730
And this is where it depends
really what you want to do.

00:34:57.730 --> 00:35:02.710
The way we see our customers
and partners using the services,

00:35:02.710 --> 00:35:04.510
and running on
top of their data,

00:35:04.510 --> 00:35:07.000
is kind of one of two buckets.

00:35:07.000 --> 00:35:09.130
One bucket I would roughly
call the analytics,

00:35:09.130 --> 00:35:11.860
and the other one would be
the machine learning side.

00:35:11.860 --> 00:35:15.220
So in the analytics
space, this word

00:35:15.220 --> 00:35:17.380
is probably one of
the more overused,

00:35:17.380 --> 00:35:18.970
especially in a
conference like this.

00:35:18.970 --> 00:35:21.040
So this really depends,
like what is the question

00:35:21.040 --> 00:35:22.210
you're trying to answer?

00:35:22.210 --> 00:35:25.510
Like for example, if you
have an expensive resource,

00:35:25.510 --> 00:35:28.770
like a physician, or a
certain piece of equipment,

00:35:28.770 --> 00:35:31.240
that is very scarce,
and that creates

00:35:31.240 --> 00:35:32.990
a bottleneck for your system.

00:35:32.990 --> 00:35:36.010
You might be interested to
see, how can I maybe overbook

00:35:36.010 --> 00:35:36.910
that resource?

00:35:36.910 --> 00:35:39.550
How many people have
ever in a flight

00:35:39.550 --> 00:35:42.160
in an airplane, or in
the airport, and you've

00:35:42.160 --> 00:35:44.830
been offered like, hey,
if you have flexibility,

00:35:44.830 --> 00:35:47.350
we will give you a
hotel or maybe $500

00:35:47.350 --> 00:35:50.115
so that you can stay and
take the next flight?

00:35:50.115 --> 00:35:51.490
So imagine you
could do something

00:35:51.490 --> 00:35:54.730
similar with an appointment,
doctor's appointment,

00:35:54.730 --> 00:35:58.150
or a specialized
MRI appointment.

00:35:58.150 --> 00:36:01.510
So this could be an example
of an analytic query.

00:36:01.510 --> 00:36:04.270
So BigQuery in this context
is one of our products

00:36:04.270 --> 00:36:07.090
that enables you to
analyze large scale

00:36:07.090 --> 00:36:09.250
data, petabyte scale data.

00:36:09.250 --> 00:36:11.980
It's an enterprise data
warehouse implementation.

00:36:11.980 --> 00:36:14.350
So the other piece
I would mention here

00:36:14.350 --> 00:36:16.360
is de-identification,
especially if you're

00:36:16.360 --> 00:36:19.060
trying to do some sort of
research collaboration.

00:36:19.060 --> 00:36:21.850
You have clinical data that has
protected health information

00:36:21.850 --> 00:36:24.610
like names and addresses.

00:36:24.610 --> 00:36:27.220
It has also, a lot
of times things that

00:36:27.220 --> 00:36:29.480
are not necessarily
immediately identifiable,

00:36:29.480 --> 00:36:31.780
but can help you narrow down.

00:36:31.780 --> 00:36:34.960
Imagine you're writing, oh
a mother of 10 children,

00:36:34.960 --> 00:36:36.040
in a town.

00:36:36.040 --> 00:36:39.580
Or the CEO of such a
company came to visit today.

00:36:39.580 --> 00:36:40.120
Right?

00:36:40.120 --> 00:36:43.690
So this information is
not directly identifying,

00:36:43.690 --> 00:36:47.140
but it can lead to
exposure of PHI.

00:36:47.140 --> 00:36:52.220
So we have tools that enable you
to de-identify data, text data,

00:36:52.220 --> 00:36:55.910
medical imaging data, that
are built in cloud services.

00:36:55.910 --> 00:37:00.170
Now this problem
specifically, we

00:37:00.170 --> 00:37:01.630
were very interested
to solve this,

00:37:01.630 --> 00:37:03.005
because this is
something that we

00:37:03.005 --> 00:37:05.560
see every research team
reinventing the wheel

00:37:05.560 --> 00:37:06.940
and building their own.

00:37:06.940 --> 00:37:08.560
And there is always
questions I get.

00:37:08.560 --> 00:37:14.260
Well can your tool detect
a pacemaker serial number

00:37:14.260 --> 00:37:15.920
in a CT image?

00:37:15.920 --> 00:37:17.893
So in this case it probably can.

00:37:17.893 --> 00:37:19.810
There is always, the
next questions like, well

00:37:19.810 --> 00:37:22.450
can it detect the name of
a patient that is written

00:37:22.450 --> 00:37:24.820
on a grain of rice in Sanskrit?

00:37:24.820 --> 00:37:28.090
Because that's a patient that
I had, that came to a CT.

00:37:28.090 --> 00:37:29.920
And the challenge I
want to bring up here

00:37:29.920 --> 00:37:32.170
is that we have data
that shows that even when

00:37:32.170 --> 00:37:34.760
people are doing
this task manually,

00:37:34.760 --> 00:37:36.520
they will still make mistakes.

00:37:36.520 --> 00:37:38.500
So you cannot devise
a perfect tool,

00:37:38.500 --> 00:37:42.190
but so the path we chose
is we implemented our tool,

00:37:42.190 --> 00:37:45.490
and we also connected
existing open-source tools,

00:37:45.490 --> 00:37:47.900
so that we can offer
our customers a choice.

00:37:47.900 --> 00:37:50.230
And you can compare and
contrast on your data,

00:37:50.230 --> 00:37:52.650
because that's
the ultimate test.

00:37:52.650 --> 00:37:55.310
A couple of things on the
natural language processing.

00:37:55.310 --> 00:37:56.920
So we're working
with customers that

00:37:56.920 --> 00:38:01.930
are solving challenges like a
trauma patient is rolling in.

00:38:01.930 --> 00:38:04.420
I have three different
radiology reports,

00:38:04.420 --> 00:38:09.820
because they had been taken to
a head CT, and a spinal X-ray,

00:38:09.820 --> 00:38:12.430
and another lumbar CT.

00:38:12.430 --> 00:38:14.410
And these, because of
the different machines,

00:38:14.410 --> 00:38:15.827
different rooms,
they were written

00:38:15.827 --> 00:38:16.930
by three different people.

00:38:16.930 --> 00:38:20.450
Can I devise a summary that
will very quickly tell me

00:38:20.450 --> 00:38:21.700
what do I need to worry about?

00:38:21.700 --> 00:38:24.350
What is the most risky
thing for this patient?

00:38:24.350 --> 00:38:28.780
So this is leveraging
heavily NLP capabilities.

00:38:28.780 --> 00:38:31.100
Anytime you want to use any
type of machine learning,

00:38:31.100 --> 00:38:32.860
you want to have labeled data.

00:38:32.860 --> 00:38:34.930
So we have solutions
for enabling

00:38:34.930 --> 00:38:38.500
you to label the data that you
already have, to create labels

00:38:38.500 --> 00:38:40.810
and to train AI models.

00:38:40.810 --> 00:38:42.640
Speaking of AI
models, here there

00:38:42.640 --> 00:38:46.550
is different categories
of applications.

00:38:46.550 --> 00:38:48.970
So some applications
are kind of operational,

00:38:48.970 --> 00:38:51.390
like help me predict
when I'm going

00:38:51.390 --> 00:38:54.670
to have a spike in demand,
so that I can anticipate,

00:38:54.670 --> 00:38:57.550
that and I can maybe
invite more nurses.

00:38:57.550 --> 00:39:00.670
Like if I have an epidemic,
or a flu epidemic,

00:39:00.670 --> 00:39:03.070
or if I have, for
example, bad weather.

00:39:03.070 --> 00:39:06.250
More people can end up end
up happening in the ER.

00:39:06.250 --> 00:39:08.420
So these are kind of on
the operational side.

00:39:08.420 --> 00:39:10.270
Another operational
example of analytics

00:39:10.270 --> 00:39:14.230
is I'm putting a patient in
the medical imaging machine.

00:39:14.230 --> 00:39:17.200
I want to make sure that
they are positioned properly,

00:39:17.200 --> 00:39:19.180
so that I don't need
to call them again,

00:39:19.180 --> 00:39:21.980
because the test was done badly.

00:39:21.980 --> 00:39:24.760
So this is an example
where the AI can help

00:39:24.760 --> 00:39:26.920
me identify the positioning.

00:39:26.920 --> 00:39:29.770
There is also the other side,
which is clinical applications.

00:39:29.770 --> 00:39:32.500
These are much more complex,
require of course more

00:39:32.500 --> 00:39:34.600
validation to go through FDA.

00:39:34.600 --> 00:39:37.120
We have customers and
partners developing these.

00:39:37.120 --> 00:39:39.580
As well, some are using
some of our tools.

00:39:39.580 --> 00:39:42.220
So a couple of tools that
I want to point out here.

00:39:42.220 --> 00:39:46.430
AutoML is a tool that allows
you to very quickly develop

00:39:46.430 --> 00:39:50.850
AI models, with relatively
limited AI expertise.

00:39:50.850 --> 00:39:53.520
So if you have a team
that has access to data,

00:39:53.520 --> 00:39:56.930
we can show you how to
use AutoML to train models

00:39:56.930 --> 00:39:58.940
on your data.

00:39:58.940 --> 00:40:01.640
If you have a team that has
a little bit more expertise

00:40:01.640 --> 00:40:03.710
and you want to
fine tune the model,

00:40:03.710 --> 00:40:05.780
then the Cloud Machine
Learning Engine

00:40:05.780 --> 00:40:07.770
might be a better tool for that.

00:40:07.770 --> 00:40:10.300
So naturally, I would
not expect AutoML

00:40:10.300 --> 00:40:13.100
to produce clinical
prediction value models.

00:40:13.100 --> 00:40:16.340
But with combination of using
AutoML and using Cloud Machine

00:40:16.340 --> 00:40:18.500
Learning Engine for
fine tuning, you

00:40:18.500 --> 00:40:21.480
can get something
that can be useful.

00:40:21.480 --> 00:40:23.270
And then finally
on the ML piece,

00:40:23.270 --> 00:40:25.248
I'll mention that one of
the challenges that we

00:40:25.248 --> 00:40:27.290
see in the space is that
a lot of people building

00:40:27.290 --> 00:40:28.130
AI applications.

00:40:28.130 --> 00:40:31.123
Some companies literally have
like one or two algorithms,

00:40:31.123 --> 00:40:32.540
and they are trying
to kind of see

00:40:32.540 --> 00:40:34.640
if there is a market for it.

00:40:34.640 --> 00:40:38.220
So we chose kind of
a meta approach here,

00:40:38.220 --> 00:40:42.110
and what I mean by that is that
we are building a tool that

00:40:42.110 --> 00:40:45.370
would enable you to evaluate
different types of AI models.

00:40:45.370 --> 00:40:47.150
So if you have a
model that claims

00:40:47.150 --> 00:40:50.277
to be able to detect
brain bleed, for example,

00:40:50.277 --> 00:40:51.860
and there is another
model that claims

00:40:51.860 --> 00:40:54.278
to be able to do the same,
how do you compare them?

00:40:54.278 --> 00:40:56.070
How do you measure the
performance of them?

00:40:58.760 --> 00:41:01.370
On the application
side, I mentioned

00:41:01.370 --> 00:41:04.430
a couple of open-source
tools that we've integrated

00:41:04.430 --> 00:41:05.930
and enabled researchers to use.

00:41:05.930 --> 00:41:07.940
These are some
open-source viewers,

00:41:07.940 --> 00:41:09.158
that you can run on top.

00:41:09.158 --> 00:41:10.700
If your store your
data on our cloud,

00:41:10.700 --> 00:41:13.040
you can use these viewers
and visualize it, presented

00:41:13.040 --> 00:41:15.050
for your research purposes.

00:41:15.050 --> 00:41:17.082
These are some of the
de-identification tools

00:41:17.082 --> 00:41:17.790
that I mentioned.

00:41:17.790 --> 00:41:20.750
So MIRC CTP and DICOM
cleaner, in the context

00:41:20.750 --> 00:41:21.920
of medical imaging.

00:41:21.920 --> 00:41:23.870
This is kind of
the best practice.

00:41:23.870 --> 00:41:25.310
These tools have
been [INAUDIBLE]

00:41:25.310 --> 00:41:26.900
more than a decade ago.

00:41:26.900 --> 00:41:29.480
But this is still what you
see, encounter in practice,

00:41:29.480 --> 00:41:32.760
when people are trying to
de-identify medical data.

00:41:32.760 --> 00:41:35.060
So what we have done is
we've built our tool,

00:41:35.060 --> 00:41:37.820
we integrated these
tools with our back end,

00:41:37.820 --> 00:41:39.650
and we offer to our
customers and partners

00:41:39.650 --> 00:41:44.000
to select them, and see
what works for them best.

00:41:44.000 --> 00:41:48.102
This is just a screenshot
from the AI model browser

00:41:48.102 --> 00:41:48.810
that I mentioned.

00:41:48.810 --> 00:41:52.190
The idea would be here that
you can select an application,

00:41:52.190 --> 00:41:54.080
and select a data
set, and you can

00:41:54.080 --> 00:41:57.560
evaluate, run this application
on top of a data set,

00:41:57.560 --> 00:41:59.890
and evaluate, compare to
specific applications.

00:42:02.530 --> 00:42:04.590
We also want to
educate the community

00:42:04.590 --> 00:42:07.210
and help people develop
their own AI applications,

00:42:07.210 --> 00:42:10.170
so we have published several
tutorials on how to do that.

